              %li
                %a{:href => "#related_concepts"} Related concepts
              %li
                %a{:href => "#references"} References
          %h2#idea Idea
          %h3#general_idea General idea
          %p
            In
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/physics"} physics
            the term
            %em theory
            or
            %em physical theory
            traditionally refers, somewhat vaguely, to a given set of notions and rules, usually formulated in the language of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mathematics"} mathematics
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/physical+system"} physical system
            or class of physical systems behaves. Typically these systems are highly idealized, in that the theories describe only certain aspects.
          %p
            Often a given such theory depends on many free parameters. When a choice of such parameters is made or the range of the parameters is being restricted one tends to call the result a
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+%28in+theoretical+physics%29"} model (in theoretical physics)
            %em
              %a{:href => "#TheoriesAndTheirModels"} Theories and their Models
            below.
          %p
            The most accurate general theory of fundamental physics known is
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Einstein+gravity"} Einstein gravity
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+field+theory"} quantum field theory
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/phenomenology"} phenomenology
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+%28in+theoretical+physics%29"} models
            which accordingly are called the
            = succeed ":" do
              %em standard models
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/standard+model+of+particle+physics"} standard model of particle physics
            and the
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/standard+model+of+cosmology"} standard model of cosmology
          %h3#formalization Formalization
          %p
            Beware that, therefore, the use of the terms
            %em theory
            and
            %em model
            in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/physics"} physics
            is
            %em different
            from the same terms as used in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/logic"} logic
            (see at
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/theory+%28logic%29"} theory (logic)
            and
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+%28logic%29"} model (logic)
          %p
            But most theories of fundamental physics (and many theories of effective physics such as
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/solid+state+physics"} solid state physics
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiom"} axiomatized
            at least to some extent:
          %p
            these physical theories are specified by a (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/local+Lagrangian"}> local
            \/
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/extended+Lagrangian"}> extended
            )
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lagrangian"} Lagrangian
            on a space of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+%28physics%29"} fields
            over a given
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacetime"} spacetime
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/worldvolume"} worldvolume
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/manifold"} manifold
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action+functional"} action functional
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi Fields
                  %msub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi H
                  %mo →
                  %mi U
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  S \;\colon\; [X, \mathbf{Fields}]_{\mathbf{H}} \to U(1)
                  \\,.
          %p
            In particular the corresponding
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+field+theory"} classical field theory
            has as its “space of models” the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/critical+locus"} critical locus
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %munder
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                    %mrow
                      %mi ϕ
                      %mo ∈
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mi X
                      %mo ,
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mi Fields
                      %msub
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                        %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                          %mi H
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi d
                  %msub
                    %mi S
                    %mi ϕ
                  %mo ≃
                  %mn 0
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\underset{\phi \in [X, \mathbf{Fields}]_{\mathbf{H}}}{\sum}
                  ( \mathbf{d} S_\phi \simeq 0 )
          %p
            of such an
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action+functional"} action functional
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Euler-Lagrange+equations"} Euler-Lagrange equations
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+%28physics%29"} fields
            in this theory, disregarding
            = succeed "-corrections," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+field+theory"} quantum field theory
          %p
            In this perspective of classical field theory, two different action functionals on different spaces of fields but with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence"} equivalent
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/critical+loci"} critical loci
            are regarded as “equivalent physical theories”. One often sees the term “classically equivalent” for this notion used in the literature.
          %p
            But the full
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+field+theory"} quantum field theory
            determined by a
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lagrangian"} Lagrangian
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action+functional"} action functional
            depends on more than just the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/critical+locus"} critical locus
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/deformation+quantization"} deformation quantization
            in terms of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/power+series"} power series
            developments in
            = succeed ".)" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Planck%27s+constant"} Planck's constant
          %h2#examples Examples
          %h3#general_principles General principles
          %p
            One broad way of classifying physical theories is by the extent to which they take
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+physics"} quantum physics
            into account. We have
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+field+theory"} classical field theory
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/prequantum+field+theory"} prequantum field theory
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+field+theory"} quantum field theory
          %p
            Here
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+field+theory"} quantum field theory
            is the most refined framework, which underlies the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/standard+model+of+particle+physics"} standard model of particle physics
          %p
            The notion of quantum field theory, fundamental as it is, is quite flexible and in particular it naturally captures the concept that a given quantum field theory only describes phenomena that occur below a certain
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/energy"} energy
            range and treats all phenomena at higher energy as the average over an unspecified more refined theory. This is the notion of
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/effective+quantum+field+theory"}> effective quantum field theory
              \.
          %p
            Crucially,
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Einstein+gravity"} Einstein gravity
            is not known to have a formulation as a
            %em fundamental
            quantum field theory with finitely many unspecified parameters (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/renormalization"}> renormalizable
            ). But it may well be a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/effective+quantum+field+theory"} effective quantum field theory
            = succeed ".)" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+gravity"} quantum gravity
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+theory"} string theory
          %h3#types_of_theories Types of theories
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/free+field+theory"} free field theory
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+theory"} gauge theory
          %h3#the_fundamental_phenomenological_theories The fundamental phenomenological theories
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gravity"}> gravity
                ,
